			"text": "Order The queries' order is the order of their entries in the rail and their chips on the Work Items page. Drag a query by its grip, or focus the grip and press Alt+↑ / Alt+↓; the new order is saved with Save. Query ID and Name Use the saved TFS query ID from its URL and choose the label shown in the rail and the Work Items header. Severity threshold Shows Critical and High counts in the Work Items header. Once their combined total reaches the threshold they are colored as ⊗ Critical Count and ⚠ High Count, and those items count on the query's rail badge. Clear to hide severity indicators. Priority threshold Shows Priority 1 and 2 counts in the Work Items header. Once their combined total reaches the threshold they are colored as ‼ Priority 1 Count and ! Priority 2 Count, and those items count on the query's rail badge. Clear to hide priority indicators. Old threshold Counts items unchanged for more than this many days and displays as ⟳ Old Count; they also count on the query's rail badge. Clear to hide. Active within When greater than 0, filters query results to items you revised within that many days. Clear to show all. A TFS query can only ask about an item's latest revision (Changed Date, Changed By = @Me), so it can't answer \"did I touch this in the last N days\" - an item you worked yesterday drops out the moment someone else edits it, and an item you never touched shows up because you happened to be the last one in. Active Within instead reads each item's full revision history and keeps the item if any revision in the window is yours. That accuracy costs one extra API call per item in the query. Every query makes that call eventually - it is the same read that fills in comments for the filter - but for the others it happens in the background after the grid is on screen, while this setting has to wait for it before the grid can be drawn at all. Answers are cached per item and reused until the item's changed date moves, which makes repeat refreshes nearly free, but the first run on a large query is noticeably slower - leave it clear unless the query is one you filter down this way. The query's severity, priority, old, and tag counts are all computed after this filter, so they describe your active items rather than the whole query. Hide query when no work items present Leaves the query out of the rail while it has no items; it comes back as soon as the query finds one. The query you have open stays until you open another. On for a new query. A query saved before this option existed stays in the rail until you turn it on. Exclude from What's Happening Keeps this query's items out of the Dashboard's What's Happening list. Its rail badge still counts them. Off for a new query."
