			"section": "help",
			"page": "settings",
			"anchor": "tools-repository-status",
			"title": "Settings",
			"heading": "Tools > Repository Status",
			"text": "Repository Roots Folders searched for Git repositories, nested ones included. Each root has an optional name (the Show list on the Repository Status page offers {name} Repos, or the folder name when it has none) and a path; add and remove rows with the shared folder picker and the trash button. A missing root is reported without stopping the others. Changing the roots rescans on Save, and Rescan now under Background Checks rescans the saved roots and patterns at any time, for example to pick up a new clone when Check local status is off. The order of the roots is the order repositories are listed in on the page: drag a root by its grip, or focus the grip and press Alt+↑ / Alt+↓. Reordering alone does not rescan. Defaults: C:\\BTR, C:\\Windows\\System32\\drivers\\etc and Visual Studio's XML schemas folder. Ignore patterns One case-insensitive .NET regular expression per line, tested against each folder path; a match skips that folder and everything under it. An invalid pattern is named under the box and nothing is saved on this page until it is fixed. Changing the patterns rescans on Save. Check local status / Every (seconds) How often each checkout's branch, changes and incoming and outgoing counts are read from disk. On, every 120 seconds (2 minutes), by default; 10 to 3,600 seconds. A value you saved before keeps its setting. Fetch upstream remotes / Every (minutes) How often the upstream remote of each checked-out branch is fetched. On, every 30 minutes, by default; 1 to 1,440 minutes. A value you saved before keeps its setting. Fetching never pulls or changes files. With either switch off, F5 still checks listed repositories without rescanning roots; Ctrl+F5 fetches them. Rescan now rediscovers the saved roots. Display remote branches Shows remote branches as child rows when a repository is expanded. Off by default; local branches and linked worktrees remain available. Confirm Push, Pull, Sync and Worktree Delete Each selected Git action asks before it runs. All are on by default and independent; a prompt's opt-out turns its switch off here, and you can turn it back on. Pull, Push and Sync ask one question naming the repository or how many there are. Commit always opens its review with the message field and changed-file list. A force push has no setting: it always opens its review and always carries its lease. With Confirm Worktree Delete off, deletes that lose no work just run; a delete that discards uncommitted files, abandons an unfinished operation, is on a detached HEAD, overrides a lock or is not proven merged into main still opens its review, and every check (unchanged since the review, and, without force, unlocked, clean and a reason to go) still applies; see Deleting worktrees. Commit message Text the Commit review's message box starts with, for example a summary-line template. Blank, the default, starts it empty. You can edit it in the review before committing."
