			"title": "Work Items",
			"heading": "View Query Results",
			"text": "Select a query under Work Items in the rail to view, filter, and sort its results. The section header takes the query's name and shows how many items it has, followed by one chip per indicator the query counts - see Settings for the thresholds. Clicking Work Items itself opens the page; its caret shows or hides the list of queries. Notable Features The open query's own actions - Return Triaged (see below) and View Query In TFS - sit in the header beside the lookup, and its filter box and Tags picker sit on the row beneath. In a narrow window the lookup and those actions move into the header's ⋯ menu. Indicator chips filter. Click a chip beside the item count to show only the items it counts; click it again to stop. Several chips together show items matching any of them, and combined with the Tags picker and the filter box, rows must match all of them. A chip whose count drops to zero stops filtering. Each query keeps its own filter text, tags, chips and sort, so switching back to a query brings them back. A query set to Hide query when no work items present is left out of the rail while it is empty, unless it is the query you have open. The header's ↻ re-runs every query at once (Ctrl+Shift+W); Ctrl+Shift+Q re-runs just the open one. Each row's ⋯ button, before its ID, opens a menu of direct actions on that work item. Sort by clicking column headings. Quick filter - a case-insensitive contains match across the title, description, tags, assignee, assigned by, and comments. Descriptions and comments are not in the original query - they are fetched in the background once the grid is on screen. While that is still running, typing in the filter shows a note saying which fields are not being searched yet. The Tags picker lists every tag in the query with how many items carry it, KAT tags first. Tick several to show items carrying any of them, or choose All for items with any tag or Missing for items with none. 'Click to copy' work item IDs to easily share/commit Assigned By names whoever handed the item over - or the person who created it if it was never reassigned. It answers \"who is waiting on me for this\". Opening a query re-runs it. The grid paints instantly from what was already fetched, then auto-corrects itself if the underlying data has changed; the rail shows animated dots on the query while it checks. The same happens if a background re-check found something. Every query's rail badge updates - even a query you are not looking at still tells you when something changed. See Reading the Dashboard for what the badges count. See Keyboard Shortcuts for navigation and action shortcuts."
