			"text": "The following eligibility rules apply for cleanup in order: Newest release (Anchor) is never touched. Releases where every environment was successfully deployed are never touched (can use Ad Hoc Delete). Releases covered by any newer release are marked as Cancel + Delete. Covered means all a newer release has exactly matching environment(s) deployed or is fully complete (meant to handle renames like PROD -> PROD Group 1/PROD Group 2). Releases where a newer release exists, but the release is not covered are marked as Cancel . Active environments are canceled with the assumption that a newer release will eventually be the production release candidate; the release is not deleted since it is only assumption and not fact. Canceled environments can always be redeployed. Two further rules limit what you may do, as opposed to what the pipeline allows. A stage waiting on an approval you are not an approver for is never cancelled - cancelling it ends the release's run at that gate, which is the approver's decision. And a release cannot be deleted, by cleanup or by Ad Hoc Delete, if it published to any environment you have no approval authority over. Authority comes from the release definition's approver roster for that environment, plus the definition owner's override; environments whose gate is automated (typically DEV) are exempt, as are environments this release never reached. Where a release carries an environment the definition no longer has - an old PROD against today's PROD Group 1 - there is no roster to read, and only the person who actually approved that stage qualifies."
