			"text": "A linked worktree's … menu has Delete Worktree…, and a repository's menu has Delete Worktrees… for all of its linked worktrees that can be deleted; the Delete Worktrees batch action deletes the ones you tick in the table. Delete is offered for a worktree whose status could be read and for one whose folder is missing; it is never offered for the primary checkout. A timer, Fetch or F5 never deletes anything. When required, the review opens at once from the last status check, which also records each linked worktree's lock and whether its branch is merged into main; it runs no Git when it opens, so anything that changed since that check is caught when the delete runs (below). The branch is never deleted: it stays in the repository with its commits. A worktree is deleted without force when it is unlocked, has nothing uncommitted (untracked files count), has no unfinished Git operation and has a reason to go: its branch is merged into the local main branch, or its upstream branch is confirmed deleted. Other removable worktrees require a forced-delete review; worktrees that cannot be checked are blocked. A worktree whose folder is missing has only its registration deleted (git worktree remove on just that path), so other missing registrations in the repository are left alone; it runs only while Git still lists that registration as missing and unlocked. \"Merged into main\" means the branch is reachable from the local main branch; with no local main, only the deleted-upstream reason can apply. A branch created from main that has no commits of its own counts as merged. A detached HEAD has no branch to check, so it is deleted with force. An upstream counts as deleted from the last fetch: fetches prune, so a branch deleted on the remote loses its remote-tracking branch, and the review treats it as a plain delete without contacting the remote. Use Ctrl+F5 to fetch first if the remote changed recently. When you confirm, the review stays open with its buttons disabled while each such upstream is checked with the remote itself (git ls-remote --exit-code for the exact branch the local branch tracks); only a definite not found counts. A branch that is back, an authentication error, a timeout or an unreachable network refuses that worktree on its own row, and the rest run. Before each delete the app checks everything again under the repository's lock: the worktree is still listed, is not the primary checkout, its folder is still there, and its branch and HEAD are the ones you saw. A worktree that moved on since the review is refused on its own row, forced or not. Without force it must also still be unlocked and clean and still have its reason; with force those are waived and Git is given --force (twice for a locked worktree). A worktree that cannot be deleted, for example because its status could no longer be read, and Git's own refusals (for example a worktree with submodules) are reported on its row; the others still run. The rows then update to match, and a row that failed stays expanded with its message. The review for one worktree names it; the review for several lists them, all ticked, and you can untick any you want to keep. Each worktree requiring force has its own warning in the list: red when it discards uncommitted files, abandons an unfinished Git operation or can leave detached commits unreachable, yellow when it only removes an unmerged branch's worktree or overrides a lock. Every delete review starts on Cancel. A review that includes a forced delete always opens, even with Confirm Worktree Delete off, cannot be confirmed with Ctrl+Enter and has no opt-out. Otherwise the review opens while Confirm Worktree Delete is on (the default), and its Don't ask me to confirm worktree deletes that lose no work switch takes effect only if you confirm; Settings turns it back on. With it off, deletes that lose no work just run."
